---
import DocShell from '../layouts/doc-shell.astro';
import { docsLinks } from '../lib/nav';

const toc = [
   { id: 'signals', label: 'Detected structures' },
   { id: 'states', label: 'Assessment states' },
   { id: 'commands', label: 'Running the scan' },
   { id: 'limits', label: 'Limits' },
];
---

<DocShell
   title="Relevant criteria"
   summary="a1 audit reads a page's HTML for forms, dialogs, headings, landmarks, live regions, media, and other structures, and lists the success criteria from the Web Content Accessibility Guidelines (WCAG) that structure makes relevant. It narrows 86 criteria to the ones worth testing on this page. It does not say pass or fail."
   links={docsLinks}
   toc={toc}
>
   <section class="panel" id="signals">
      <h2>Detected structures</h2>
      <p>
         <code>a1 audit</code> fetches the page's HTML alongside its axe run and accessibility tree,
         then scans that HTML for 15 kinds of structure: <code>auth</code>, <code>dialog</code>,
         <code>drag-and-drop</code>, <code>form</code>, <code>heading</code>, <code>help</code>,
         <code>landmark</code>, <code>live-region</code>, <code>media</code>, <code>menu</code>,
         <code>overlay</code>, <code>repeated-form</code>, <code>tablist</code>,
         <code>validation</code>, and <code>widget</code>.
      </p>
      <p>
         Each match is a signal in the JSON output. It has the category, the source it came
         from (the DOM, the accessibility tree, document metadata, a W3C Quickref tag, or a hint you
         supplied), the text that matched, a confidence of <code>high</code>, <code>medium</code>,
         or <code>low</code>, and up to ten <code>elements</code>: the XPath, tag, and opening tag
         of the nodes that produced it.
      </p>
      <pre data-label="OUTPUT"><code>"signals": [
  &#123;
    "category": "heading", "source": "dom", "value": "heading structure", "confidence": "high",
    "elements": [&#123; "xpath": "/html/body/main/h1", "tag": "h1", "snippet": "&lt;h1&gt;" &#125;]
  &#125;,
  &#123;
    "category": "form", "source": "dom", "value": "form controls", "confidence": "high",
    "elements": [
      &#123; "xpath": "/html/body/main/form", "tag": "form", "snippet": "&lt;form action=\"/save\" method=\"post\"&gt;" &#125;,
      &#123; "xpath": "/html/body/main/form/label/input", "tag": "input", "snippet": "&lt;input type=\"text\" name=\"displayName\"&gt;" &#125;
    ]
  &#125;
]</code></pre>
   </section>

   <section class="ledger" id="states">
      <h2>Assessment states</h2>
      <p>
         Every success criterion has a topic tag list from the W3C Quickref, such as
         <code>forms</code>, <code>modals</code>, or <code>focus</code>. The scan matches each
         signal's category against those tags and gives every criterion one state:
      </p>
      <div class="route-list">
         <div class="route-row">
            <code>applicable</code>
            <span>A signal matches the criterion's tags with enough confidence. Test this criterion on this page.</span>
         </div>
         <div class="route-row">
            <code>not-detected</code>
            <span>No signal matched. The criterion may still be relevant, because the scan reads only the HTML the server returned.</span>
         </div>
         <div class="route-row">
            <code>out-of-scope</code>
            <span>The criterion does not apply to this kind of page.</span>
         </div>
         <div class="route-row">
            <code>unknown</code>
            <span>The signals do not decide it either way.</span>
         </div>
      </div>
   </section>

   <section class="panel" id="commands">
      <h2>Running the scan</h2>
      <p>
         The text output of <code>a1 audit</code> combines the assessment with each criterion's
         <a href="/test-methods">test method</a>: the criteria that are relevant to the page and
         that <code>a1 axe</code> cannot decide are listed under <code>Check by hand</code>. The
         output below comes from a page with one heading and a button that opens a Preferences
         dialog.
      </p>
      <pre><code>a1 audit http://localhost:3000/</code></pre>
      <pre data-label="OUTPUT"><code>audit  http://localhost:3000/

✓ Nothing failed the automated checks.

Check by hand
  26 criteria this page triggers need a person:
    • 1.3.2  Meaningful Sequence
    • 1.3.3  Sensory Characteristics
    • 2.1.2  No Keyboard Trap
    • 2.1.4  Character Key Shortcuts
    • 2.4.1  Bypass Blocks
    • 2.4.3  Focus Order
    ...

  Read the page as a screen reader does:  a1 sr walk http://localhost:3000/

The page
  Titled Dialog demo, opening with Dialog demo.
  It has 1 landmark, 1 heading (level 1), no links, 1 button, no form controls.

86 criteria were considered. Add --verbose for every one, or --json for the full report.</code></pre>
      <p>
         With <code>--json</code>, the result has a <code>relevance</code> section with
         one assessment per criterion. The MCP server returns the same assessments from its
         <code>audit</code> tool.
      </p>
      <pre><code>a1 audit http://localhost:3000/ --json</code></pre>
      <pre data-label="OUTPUT"><code>"relevance": &#123;
  "matrix": &#123;
    "assessments": &#123;
      "2.4.3": &#123;
        "criterionId": "2.4.3",
        "title": "Focus Order",
        "state": "applicable",
        "reasons": [
          "Detected landmark, heading, form, dialog structure, and fixed or overlay signals (landmark structure, heading structure, form controls, and dialog structure) and matching criterion tags (navigation, structure, forms, controls, errors, modals, focus, keyboard, and menus)."
        ],
        "matchedSignalCategories": ["landmark", "heading", "form", "dialog", "overlay"],
        "matchedTags": ["navigation", "structure", "forms", "controls", "errors", "modals", "focus", "keyboard", "menus"],
        "elements": [
          &#123; "xpath": "/html/body/main", "tag": "main", "snippet": "&lt;main&gt;" &#125;,
          &#123; "xpath": "/html/body/main/h1", "tag": "h1", "snippet": "&lt;h1&gt;" &#125;,
          &#123; "xpath": "/html/body/div/div[2]", "tag": "div", "snippet": "&lt;div role=\"dialog\" aria-modal=\"true\" aria-label=\"Preferences\"&gt;" &#125;
        ]
      &#125;
    &#125;
  &#125;
&#125;</code></pre>
      <p>
         The dialog is hidden until a button click, but its markup is in the HTML, so the
         <code>dialog</code> signal counts before the click. <code>reasons</code> lists the signals
         and the tags that produced the state. <code>elements</code> lists the nodes behind it,
         merged across the matched signals and capped at 20. Paste an XPath into the browser
         console as <code>$x('/html/body/main')</code> to select the node. The text output prints
         the first few with <code>--verbose</code>.
      </p>
   </section>

   <section class="field-note" id="limits">
      <h2>Limits</h2>
      <p>
         An assessment never says pass or fail. It says which of the 86 criteria are worth testing
         on this page.
      </p>
      <p>
         The scan reads the HTML the server returned. Content a framework renders after load, or a
         dialog that only exists after a click, is not detected and leaves its criteria at
         <code>not-detected</code>. To test those states, open the page in a
         <a href="/guides/screen-reader">screen reader session</a> and move through it.
      </p>
   </section>
</DocShell>
